      ":question/problem": "Consider the matrix $A$ below. Which set of entries should be replaced by $0$ to obtain row echelon form **using the fewest replacements**? Leave all other entries unchanged.\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 5 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The minimum set is $a_{31}$. Replace this entry with $0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0 & 5 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,\\longrightarrow\\,\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -3 & -7 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5 & -6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe nonzero rows have leading entries in columns 1, 2, 3, in strictly increasing order, and all zero rows are at the bottom. \n\nRestoring $a_{31}$ would put the leading entry of row 3 in column 1, which is not to the right of the leading entry in row 2. The original matrix is not in row echelon form, so at least one replacement is needed.\n\nEntries to the right of a leading entry may remain nonzero.",
